   "text": "The model built a new schema.org module by typing it in place. The design was flawed from the first write and was never corrected. The model then got stuck in a long completion-claim loop.\n\nThe first draft of `schemaorg.py` merged every record into one flat dict with last-write-wins semantics [T0B23:<RANGE>properties.update(props)</RANGE>]. The rewrite applied JSON-LD after microdata, so JSON-LD overrides microdata [T0B31:<RANGE>self._properties.update(_parse_microdata(self._soup))\n        self._properties.update(_parse_json_ld(self._soup))</RANGE>]. That contradicts the instruction that microdata be tried first. The model's own test encoded the wrong order: it labelled a case \"microdata before json-ld\" but asserted the JSON-LD title [T0B31:<RANGE>assert p.title == \"The Article\"</RANGE>]. Microdata also never separates article-like from site-level records. Instead it renames `name` to title for any item [T0B53:<RANGE>properties[\"title\"] = properties.pop(\"name\")</RANGE>].\n\nThe model's self-written tests passed [T0B62:<RANGE>ALL TESTS PASSED</RANGE>] and it called mark_task_complete [T0B73:<RANGE>mark_task_complete()</RANGE>]. The harness asked for confirmation [T0B74:<RANGE>If so, include \"task_complete\": true in your JSON response again.</RANGE>]. The model replied with shell echoes instead [T0B75:<RANGE>echo \"task_complete\"</RANGE>], so the handshake was never completed. It kept echoing even after the harness warned it [T0B90:<RANGE>you are repeating; change approach or finish</RANGE>]. The run ended with a LoopBreakStop exception after 60 episodes and reward 0 [R0M.trace_lab].",
